# BUG WITNESS, not a benchmark: `mangleKername` is not injective, so our Java
# backend cannot compile a program that Peregrine compiles fine.
#
# Arend-authored (ExampleMangleCollision.mangleCollisionProgram): prog.ast is
# `ExamplePrint:mangleCollisionSexpr`, regenerated by
# test/tools/regen-arend-asts.sh. prog.attr is a copy of
# runtime/peano/empty.attr -- the program realizes no axioms.
#
# The mechanism. `joinDirs` (ToJava.ard:221-224) joins DirPath components with
# `"_"`, and `isJavaIdentByte` (StringUtil.ard:74-84) passes byte 95 (`_`)
# through unescaped, so a component containing `_` is indistinguishable from a
# component boundary:
#
#   mangleKername (mpFile ["A_B"],   "f")  ==  "c_A_B__f"
#   mangleKername (mpFile ["A","B"], "f")  ==  "c_A_B__f"
#
# This program declares two DIFFERENT constants at exactly those two kernames,
# with bodies `true` and `false`, and returns the first. Our backend emits two
# `public static Object c_A_B__f()` methods and javac rejects the class
# ("method c_A_B__f() is already defined in class Prog"), i.e. build-fail(java).
#
# This is the LOUD half of the mangling bug; ../axiom-mangle-collision/ is the
# silent half, which is the dangerous one.
#
# `xfail=` is deliberately EMPTY: this is a real, open bug in this repository's
# compiler, and `run.py --all` should exit non-zero until `joinDirs` uses a
# separator outside `javaIdentPart`'s output alphabet (or escapes `_` inside a
# component). It is not a limitation to be absorbed.
#
# Peregrine is unaffected ON THIS PAIR, which was checked rather than assumed --
# work/mangle-collision/ocaml/bench.mlf holds two distinct bindings:
#
#   mpFile ["A_B"]   "f"  ->  $def_A_B_f
#   mpFile ["A","B"] "f"  ->  $def_B_A_f      (components in REVERSE order)
#
# Note, though, that metarocq-malfunction's own mangling has the same weakness,
# just on a different pair: it also joins with `_`, so `mpFile ["B","A"] "f"`
# gives `$def_A_B_f` too. Feeding that variant through `peregrine ocaml` emits
#
#   (module ($def_A_B_f 0) ($def_A_B_f 1) ($def_main $def_A_B_f) ...)
#
# -- two bindings of one name, and Malfunction takes the later one SILENTLY
# rather than rejecting the module. So upstream has the quieter version of the
# same bug. That is out of scope here (this corpus tests OUR backend, and a
# λ□ program is the input, not peregrine's source), but it is the reason the
# note below says "javac rejects" rather than "only Java is affected".
expected=1
backends=java ocaml c
runtime=peano
xfail=
note=BUG: kername mangling is not injective - mpFile [A_B] and mpFile [A,B] both mangle to c_A_B__f, so javac rejects a duplicate method
